UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 1, Lesson 1: What makes something living', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Living things typically move (themselves or parts), grow, need food/energy and water, sense surroundings and reproduce. Non-living things do not do all of these.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same science idea to a new object or material, then compare two pieces of evidence for strength.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nSort: leaf on a tree; fallen dry leaf; bicycle; frog. Which are living things right now?\n\nFEEDBACK\nLeaf on tree and frog are living; dry leaf and bicycle are non-living (leaf was part of a living plant).\n\nWATCH FOR\nThinking anything that moves is living (cars) or anything green is living (plastic plant) mixes up features.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nObserve eight local items. Record living/non-living with two features as evidence for each. Write one borderline case and how you decided.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one observation or step you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Science and Technology","learning_intention":"Living things typically move (themselves or parts), grow, need food/energy and water, sense surroundings and reproduce. Non-living things do not do all of these.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Investigate","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: safe everyday objects for observation"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["ST2-SCI-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Observe eight local items. Record living/non-living with two features as evidence for each. Write one borderline case and how you decided. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original demonstration: A worm moves, grows, eats and can reproduce → living. A rock does not grow or reproduce → non-living. A wooden chair was once living material but is not a living thing now.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Thinking anything that moves is living (cars) or anything green is living (plastic plant) mixes up features. Correction: Leaf on tree and frog are living; dry leaf and bicycle are non-living (leaf was part of a living plant).\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Thinking anything that moves is living (cars) or anything green is living (plastic plant) mixes up features. Keep sample answers out of the child role.","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Living things typically move (themselves or parts), grow, need food/energy and water, sense surroundings and reproduce. Non-living things do not do all of these. The other choices avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["Living things typically move (themselves or parts), grow, need food/energy and water, sense surroundings and reproduce. Non-living things do not do all of these.","Thinking anything that moves is living (cars) or anything green is living (plastic plant) mixes up features.","Ignore the observation and invent a more exciting result.","Copy the model objects and call that independent work."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Understand and model\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Make the science idea visible: name the observation, property or test step you used and say why it supports the claim. The other choices avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["Make the science idea visible: name the observation, property or test step you used and say why it supports the claim.","Writing only a final label without evidence does not show scientific thinking.","Copy the model objects and call that independent work.","Treat a missing measurement as if it were recorded."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Show the evidence\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific science error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim. The other choices avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific science error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim.","Changing the wording without repairing the evidence does not fix the error. Thinking anything that moves is living (cars) or anything green is living (plastic plant) mixes up features.","Treat a missing measurement as if it were recorded.","Skip the fair-test idea because the first guess looked neat."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Test a misconception\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use the same science idea on a new object, material or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of observations. The other choices avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["Use the same science idea on a new object, material or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of observations.","Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new evidence.","Skip the fair-test idea because the first guess looked neat.","Ignore the observation and invent a more exciting result."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Transfer to a new investigation\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Leaf on tree and frog are living; dry leaf and bicycle are non-living (leaf was part of a living plant). Useful feedback uses the original observations and method.