Useful feedback uses the original observations and method.","options":["The new example should use the same science idea as the model, but contain different objects or data.","Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new evidence.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"Investigate or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a s\", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence, not repetition of the worked model, demonstrates the skill.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced relevant evidence with accurate reasoning and named observations.","question":"Investigate or describe a fresh example that uses the central skill. Show evidence and name the observations that matter.","sample_answer":"Original demonstration: A worm moves, grows, eats and can reproduce → living. A rock does not grow or reproduce → non-living. A wooden chair was once living material but is not a living thing now.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A correction needs to change the claim or evidence, not just make the notes longer.","marking_guide":"Require an actual misconception plus corrected claim and an evidence-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely science misconception for this topic, correct it and say why the correction works.","sample_answer":"Common error: Thinking anything that moves is living (cars) or anything green is living (plastic plant) mixes up features. Correction: Leaf on tree and frog are living; dry leaf and bicycle are non-living (leaf was part of a living plant).","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-sci-y3-w01-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA Science and Technology alignment using catalogue outcome codes; content-point mapping remains pending.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise living.","Read the mission before you start observing or testing.","Keep the property or variable you are studying in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your evidence with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the observation or step that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore observations or fair tests.","Prefer accurate evidence over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your evidence and reason are both present.","Proofread labels, measurements and sequence of steps.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact observation or test step before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key observations.","Say what each observation shows about the idea.","A final label alone is weaker than recorded evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak claim with stronger evidence.","Change the claim or method, not only how neat the drawing looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh object or context that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original evidence and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new objects or data."]},"subject":"Living World","success_criteria":["I can use living with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify non-living and explain why it matters.","I can practise observe independently on a new prompt.","I can notice feature needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Living things typically move (themselves or parts), grow, need food/energy and water, sense surroundings and reproduce. Non-living things do not do all of these. Supported records follow the actual observations and purpose of the task.","options":["Living things typically move (themselves or parts), grow, need food/energy and water, sense surroundings and reproduce. Non-living things do not do all of these.","A claim unrelated to the observation or test.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no evidence."],"question":"What should you demonstrate in this teaching stage?"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nA worm moves, grows, eats and can reproduce → living. A rock does not grow or reproduce → non-living. A wooden chair was once living material but is not a living thing now.\n\nTRY FIRST\nSort: leaf on a tree; fallen dry leaf; bicycle; frog. Which are living things right now?\n\nFEEDBACK\nLeaf on tree and frog are living; dry leaf and bicycle are non-living (leaf was part of a living plant).","explain":"Living things typically move (themselves or parts), grow, need food/energy and water, sense surroundings and reproduce. Non-living things do not do all of these.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen or measured.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Thinking anything that moves is living (cars) or anything green is living (plastic plant) mixes up features. Correct the claim or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Living vs non-living features.","caption":"Use several features, not one.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Living</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">grows, needs energy, reproduces</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Non-living</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">rock / chair</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which observation matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: A worm moves, grows, eats and can reproduce → living. A rock does not grow or reproduce → non-living. A wooden chair was Then write your own evidence.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which observation matters most.\nJuno: Name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: A worm moves, grows, eats and can reproduce → living. A rock does not grow or reproduce → non-living. A wooden chair was Then write your own evidence."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Make the science idea visible: name the observation, property or test step you used and say why it supports the claim. Supported records follow the actual observations and purpose of the task.","options":["Make the science idea visible: name the observation, property or test step you used and say why it supports the claim.","A claim unrelated to the observation or test.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no evidence."],"question":"What should you demonstrate in this teaching stage?"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nA worm moves, grows, eats and can reproduce → living. A rock does not grow or reproduce → non-living. A wooden chair was once living material but is not a living thing now. Look at each observation, variable or design choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key observations or steps from the model and say what each one shows.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful record names the evidence and the reason it supports the claim, not only a label.","explain":"Make the science idea visible: name the observation, property or test step you used and say why it supports the claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen or measured.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without evidence does not show scientific thinking. Correct the claim or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Show the evidence","visual":[{"alt":"Feature evidence beats appearance.","caption":"Green colour alone is not enoug