OW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without repairing the evidence does not fix the error. Thinking anything that moves is living (cars) or anything green is living (plastic plant) mixes up features. Correct the claim or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use the same science idea on a new object, material or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of observations. Supported records follow the actual observations and purpose of the task.","options":["Use the same science idea on a new object, material or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of observations.","A claim unrelated to the observation or test.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no evidence."],"question":"What should you demonstrate in this teaching stage?"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nLiving things typically move (themselves or parts), grow, need food/energy and water, sense surroundings and reproduce. Non-living things do not do all of these. Now choose a different example to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nInvestigate or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short evidence-based reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same science idea as the model, but contain different objects or data.","explain":"Use the same science idea on a new object, material or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of observations.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis 